---
id: UR-003
type: rule
family: unsafe-rendering
title: React dangerouslySetInnerHTML
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 3

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.test.js',
    '**/*.test.jsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.js',
    '**/*.spec.jsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: 'dangerouslySetInnerHTML'

related: [UR-001, UR-002]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, which opts the value out of
React's automatic escaping and injects it as raw HTML. React escapes
normal children by default; this prop is the deliberate bypass, and it is
the most common way XSS re-enters an otherwise-safe React app (CWE-79).

If you're rendering text or structure, pass the value as a normal child
(`<div>{value}</div>`) and let React escape it. If you genuinely need
rich HTML from dynamic input, run it through DOMPurify (or an equivalent
allowlist sanitiser) and pass only the sanitiser's output to
`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` — keep the sanitise call adjacent so review can
see it.

If the `__html` value is a static, developer-authored constant, suppress
UR-003 with a reason noting the input is not dynamic.
